latest_plan = "Based on the reflection and analysis of the examples, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Analyze the Input Grid:\n   a. Count the number of cells for each color.\n   b. Identify the largest connected region for each color.\n   c. Determine the relative positions of color regions (top, bottom, left, right, center).\n\n2. Establish Color Hierarchy:\n   a. Rank colors by their total cell count.\n   b. Typically, the order will be: Blue (1) > Red (2) > Yellow (4) > Others.\n\n3. Create the Main Structure:\n   a. Use the largest region of the dominant color (usually blue or red) as the base.\n   b. Simplify its shape into a rectangle or L-shape, maintaining its original position.\n   c. Size this region proportionally to its input size, but leave space for other colors.\n\n4. Process Secondary Colors:\n   a. Start with the second largest color region.\n   b. If it can fit inside the main structure while maintaining relative position, place it there.\n   c. If it doesn't fit inside, create an L-shape or rectangle adjacent to the main structure.\n\n5. Handle Remaining Colors:\n   For each color, from largest to smallest:\n   a. Create a rectangular region proportional to its input size.\n   b. Try to place it inside the main structure or secondary structure if possible.\n   c. If it doesn't fit inside, place it adjacent to existing structures.\n   d. Always maintain the relative position from the input (e.g., if it was on the right, keep it on the right).\n\n6. Special Color Handling:\n   a. Sky Blue (8): Try to place centrally within the main structure if possible.\n   b. Yellow (4): If not already placed, create a distinct region, often inside the main structure.\n   c. Smaller colors (Green, Magenta): Incorporate into larger structures while preserving their presence.\n\n7. Optimize Layout:\n   a. Adjust sizes of color regions to fit efficiently within the grid.\n   b. Ensure smaller color regions are represented proportionally to their input size.\n   c. Fill any gaps within color regions with the surrounding color.\n\n8. Final Adjustments:\n   a. Remove all Gray (5) dots.\n   b. Ensure all regions are rectangular and continuous.\n   c. Create a Black (0) border around the entire structure.\n\n9. Verify the Output:\n   a. Check that all colors from the input are represented in the output.\n   b. Confirm that the relative positions of color regions are maintained.\n   c. Ensure the output grid is simpler and more structured than the input.\n   d. Verify that the size of each color region is roughly proportional to its input size.\n\n10. Implement Edge Cases:\n    a. If a color appears only as isolated pixels in the input, represent it as a small rectangle in the output.\n    b. If the input is very sparse, consider creating a more compact output while maintaining color relationships.\n\n11. Iterative Refinement:\n    a. If the initial placement doesn't satisfy all conditions, iteratively adjust the layout.\n    b. Prioritize maintaining color hierarchy and relative positions over exact proportions.\n\nThis step-by-step plan should be flexible enough to handle various input configurations while consistently producing outputs that match the observed patterns in the examples. The key is to balance the preservation of relative positions and size proportions with the need for simplification and structure in the output."
